<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander McGillivray</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1750–<a href="/years/1793">1793</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander McGillivray was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a> (Creek) people, the son of a Scottish trader and a Creek mother. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a> he used alliances with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to resist encroachment by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, and in 1790 he negotiated the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-mcgillivray.jpg" alt="Alexander McGillivray" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_McGillivray.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>McGillivray was born Hopuewv Herē Mēkko, Good Child King, at the Coushatta village of Little Tallassee on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coosa-river">Coosa River</a> on 15 December 1750. His mother, Sehoy Marchand, belonged to the Wind Clan, and since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a> were matrilineal her children were Wind Clan too. His father, Lachlan McGillivray, was a trader who came to own over 10,000 acres of plantations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. In 1773 Alexander was sent to school in Charleston, where he learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek, and he was apprenticed to two trading firms. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> broke out his Loyalist father returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and lost his lands; Alexander served as a British lieutenant colonel and in 1777 returned to Little Tallassee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1783 McGillivray became principal chief of the Upper Creek towns. He opposed the Treaty of Augusta of that year, by which two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-creek">Lower Creek</a> chiefs had ceded land to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, and in June 1784 he negotiated the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, which recognised Muscogee sovereignty over three million acres claimed by Georgia and made him a Spanish agent. He became a partner in Panton, Leslie &amp; Company and used the deerskin trade to build his power, seeking to replace the autonomy of the towns with authority centred on himself. Creek war parties fought beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> from 1785 to 1787.</p><p>Under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> of 1790 the Altamaha and Oconee rivers became the boundary, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> promised to remove illegal settlers, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creeks</a> agreed to return escaped slaves. Secret clauses made McGillivray a brigadier general with a salary and paid him $100,000 for his father's lost property. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-creek">Lower Creeks</a> complained that they had not been represented, and the Creeks soon concluded that he had deceived them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1792 McGillivray repudiated the treaty and made another with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Long ill, he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a> on 17 February 1793 and was buried in William Panton's garden; his sister later had him reburied at Choctaw Bluff on the Alabama River. Early historians cast him as a hero, while later ones have called his leadership corrupt. His nephews William Weatherford and William McIntosh fought on opposite sides of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/creek-war">Creek War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexander McGillivray” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Alexander McGillivray</b>, the leading Muscogee diplomat from the 1780s, negotiated the Treaty of New York with the United States in 1790.</blockquote><a href="/state/muscogee-confederacy">Muscogee Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
